<h2>Definition List</h2>
<dl>
  <dt>HTML5</dt>
  <dd>A standardized markup language for creating web pages, defining structure and semantics.</dd>
  <dt>Semantic Tag</dt>
  <dd>An HTML element that clearly describes its meaning to both the browser and the developer (e.g., &lt;header&gt;, &lt;article&gt;).</dd>
  <dt>DOM</dt>
  <dd>Document Object Model, a programming interface for web documents.</dd>
</dl>

<h2>Accordion (No JavaScript)</h2>
<!-- Note: True accordion functionality requires JavaScript to toggle visibility. This uses CSS pseudo-elements and checkbox hacks for basic toggling without JS execution logic -->
<div class="accordion">
    <input type="checkbox" id="section1" hidden>
    <label for="section1" class="accordion-header">Section One Title</label>
    <div class="accordion-content">Content for the first collapsible section goes here.</div>

    <input type="checkbox" id="section2" hidden>
    <label for="section2" class="accordion-header">Section Two Title</label>
    <div class="accordion-content">This is the content displayed when the second section is toggled open using the checkbox hack.</div>
</div>

<style>
/* CSS Hack to make the checkbox toggle work like an accordion */
input[type="checkbox"] { display: none; }
.accordion-header {
    display: block;
}
.accordion-content {
    max-height: 0;
    overflow: hidden;
    transition: max-height 0.3s ease-out;
}

/* When the associated checkbox is checked, show the content */
#section1:checked ~ .accordion-content,
#section2:checked ~ .accordion-content {
    max-height: 500px; /* Set a large enough value to allow expansion */
}
</style>

</body>
</html>
